<section>
  <grid spacing="none">
    <grid-item colspan="38" vertical-align="center">
      <p role="eyebrow">Retrieval practice</p>
      <h2>Can you reconstruct the movement?</h2>
      <grid direction="vertical" divider="sm neutral-border" marker="number" marker-position="inline" marker-numbering-format="decimal-leading-zero" style="--marker-inline-size: 30pt; --marker-gap: 14pt" vertical-sizing="hug">
        <grid-item padding="10pt 0">
          <h4>Diagnose the claim</h4>
          <p>State the seminar’s original claim and identify its internal problem.</p>
        </grid-item>
        <grid-item padding="10pt 0">
          <h4>Track the sublation</h4>
          <p>Explain what turn-taking cancels, preserves, and transforms.</p>
        </grid-item>
        <grid-item padding="10pt 0">
          <h4>Explain recognition</h4>
          <p>Show why recognition requires another independent person.</p>
        </grid-item>
      </grid>
      <hr horizontalAlign="left" width="xs" thickness="md" color="accent"/>
      <p><b>Takeaway:</b> Hegel asks not only whether an idea is wrong, but how its own limits can disclose a more adequate form. Do not recite a formula; reconstruct the change.</p>
    </grid-item>
    <grid-item colspan="22" padding="0">
      <ai-image background="true" aspect-ratio="portrait" image-style="dialectical-paper-assemblage" prompt="Editorial cut-paper assemblage photographed on warm ivory paper (#FAF4E8), with crisp folded planes, subtle paper fiber and long soft daylight shadows, using vermilion #A13D32, blue slate #345F67 and ochre #B3822D with carbon-black details, no text or lettering. A vermilion path carries an ochre fragment through an open folded threshold and becomes two reciprocal blue-slate arcs between independent mirrored profiles."/>
    </grid-item>
  </grid>
</section>